@h1 Custom nodes / extending vocabulary h1@

Core vocab is closed: 52 reserved names, not extensible. Everything else you add
either by @strong composition strong@ — a definition, no code — or through the
@strong opaque strong@ @@@code @node code@@@ plus renderer dispatch. This page
covers both sides: what an author writes and what a renderer does with it.

@h2 Three ways to extend h2@

@ul
@li @strong A definition. strong@ @@@code #name … name# code@@@ is pure
composition; the expander inlines it, and no renderer changes. Reach for this
first. li@
@li @strong The opaque node. strong@ @@@code @node |type X| code@@@ survives
@code expand code@ as an ordinary @code NodeUse code@ (@code name: 'node' code@),
and your renderer dispatches on the @code type code@ param. Reach for this when
you need @em new rendering em@ that composition can't express. li@
@li @strong Core vocab. strong@ Fixed and @em not em@ extensible;
@code isReservedNodeName code@ rejects any redefinition. Shown here only for
contrast. li@
ul@

@h2 The opaque-node contract h2@

@@@code @node code@@@ needs a @code type code@ param; all other params and the
body survive into the expanded AST. The HTML renderer dispatches two ways:

@ul
@li @strong type is itself a core-vocab name. strong@ The renderer re-emits that
element. Verified:
@@@code @node |type img| |src ./lamp.png| |alt …| code@@@ →
@@@code <img src="./lamp.png" alt="The keeper&#39;s lamp"> code@@@. li@
@li @strong type is anything else. strong@ The renderer emits a generic
@code <div> code@ carrying @strong every named param as strong@ @code data-* code@.
Verified:
@@@code @node |type chart| |layout horizontal| A body node@ code@@@ →
@@@code <div data-type="chart" data-layout="horizontal"><p> A body </p></div> code@@@. li@
ul@

@aside(class note) ⚠️ It is @code data-type code@, not @code data-wit-type code@
— the source comment is loose. @strong Every strong@ named param, @code type code@
included, becomes a @code data-* code@ attribute. Markdown has no opaque
container, so it emits just the body. aside@

@h2 The preferred authoring pattern h2@

Raw @@@code @node code@@@ is noisy in prose. Wrap it in a definition so authors
get a natural name while the AST still carries @code type code@ for the renderer:

@@@pre
#highlight ||content||
@node |type highlight| ::content:: node@
highlight#

@highlight Some emphasised text highlight@
pre@@@

The author writes @@@code @highlight … code@@@; the expanded AST carries
@@@code type highlight code@@@ for the renderer to dispatch on. Fixture
@code 20-opaque-node/user-defined-wrapper.wit code@.

@h2 Every edge h2@

@ul
@li @strong Block form vs. self-closing. strong@ @@@code @node … node@ code@@@ has
a body; @@@code @node |type img| code@@@ self-closes. Fixtures
@code node-with-body code@ and @code bare-node-passthrough code@. li@
@li @strong Unknown types stay portable. strong@ An unknown @code type code@ is
round-trippable via @code data-* code@, so a document survives across renderers
that don't know the type. li@
@li @strong Names are unique. strong@ A duplicate non-additive @@@code #name code@@@
throws @code E_DUPLICATE_DEFINITION code@; core names cannot be redefined at all. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Trying to redefine @@@code @table code@@@ or @@@code @h1 code@@@ — they are
reserved. li@
@li Expecting Markdown to render an opaque container — it emits the body only. li@
@li Reading @code data-wit-type code@ — the attribute is @code data-type code@. li@
ul@

